If an interviewer asks "Why Qdrant over OpenSearch?"

A good answer is:

OpenSearch is excellent for log analytics and keyword search, but my primary requirement was semantic retrieval of historical incidents and runbooks. Since the Incident Memory layer is fundamentally a vector-search problem, I chose Qdrant because it is purpose-built for embeddings, simpler to operate locally, and more resource-efficient. If I needed large-scale log analytics, I would complement Qdrant with OpenSearch rather than replace it.

That answer sounds like a senior engineer making a design tradeoff rather than choosing tools based on popularity.